-- title:
--   The torus generators $Y_i Z_i - 1$ form a weakly regular sequence in $K[X, Y, Z]$
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $A_0, A_1$ types, and let $K[X, Y, Z]$ be the polynomial ring over $K$ in the variables $X_h$ ($h \in A_0$) and $Y_i, Z_i$ ($i \in A_1$). The per-coordinate box `torusBox K A₀ A₁ T`, for $T \in \mathbb{N}^{A_0 \sqcup A_1}$, is the span of the monomials $X^a Y^b Z^c$ with $a_h \le T_h$ for every $h$ and $b_i + c_i \le T_i$ for every $i$; $J_0 = (Y_i Z_i - 1)_{i \in A_1}$ is the torus ideal `torusIdeal K A₀ A₁`, the kernel of $K[X, Y, Z] \to K[\mathbb{G}_a^{A_0} \times \mathbb{G}_m^{A_1}]$; a monomial $X^a Y^b Z^c$ is reduced when $\min(b_i, c_i) = 0$ for every $i$ (`torusReduced`). Assume that $A_1$ is finite. Then the list `torusList K A₀ A₁` of the generators $Y_i Z_i - 1$ of $J_0$, one for each $i \in A_1$, is a weakly regular sequence in $K[X, Y, Z]$. A list is weakly regular on $S$ when each entry is a nonzerodivisor on the quotient of $S$ by the ideal generated by the earlier entries (Mathlib's `IsWeaklyRegular`; the final quotient may be zero). For a list $ps_0$, $(ps_0)$ is the ideal generated by its entries and $|ps_0|$ its length. The names `boxHilb`, `blockBox`, `torusBox`, `torusIdeal`, `torusList`, `torusReduced`, `boxD`, `torusCoeffPoly`, `torusPoint` and `UnmixedHeight` are those of the published Definition `DiazHilbertBox` (module `Definitions.Def_DiazHilbertBox`).
--
--   **Proof.** It suffices that $f_j = Y_j Z_j - 1$ is a nonzerodivisor modulo $I = (f_i : i \in s)$ for every $j$ and every set $s$ of indices not containing $j$. Separating the variables $Y_j, Z_j$ from the others writes $K[X, Y, Z]$ as $S[Y_j, Z_j]$, with $I = I_0 \cdot S[Y_j, Z_j]$ for an ideal $I_0$ of $S$, so the quotient is $(S/I_0)[Y_j, Z_j]$. There $Y_j Z_j - 1$ has constant coefficient $-1$, a unit, so it is a unit of the power series ring and hence a nonzerodivisor of the polynomial ring.
--
--   **Novelty.** Classical; routine formalisation.
-- source:
--   Classical commutative algebra. Compare M. Waldschmidt, Diophantine Approximation on Linear Algebraic Groups, Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften 326, Springer, 2000, Lemma 8.7 (§8.3.1; Chapter 8 is by D. Roy), which shows that the ideals of algebraic subgroups in K[X, Y, Z] are complete intersections. C1.1 of the Diaz modulus mission. Formal proof: Diaz modulus mission, 29 October 2026 (C. Perassi).

